## Why a spa cure, when what I want is to lose weight

Most diets fail not because they are bad, but because they take place in the middle of everyday life — between work, hurry, and a fridge within arm's reach. **Weight management at the spa in Mariánské Lázně (Marienbad)** works on the opposite principle: it lifts you out of your usual routine for a few weeks and places you in surroundings that nudge you towards a healthier rhythm all by themselves. A **metabolic cure** therefore promises no quick kilo lost, but something more lasting — a slow rebalancing of the metabolism, and new habits that survive the journey home.

Metabolic conditions — obesity, type 2 diabetes, gout, digestive complaints — are among the officially recognised spa indications of Mariánské Lázně. This is no niche offering but one of the pillars the local spa tradition has rested on for more than two centuries. The water of the local springs was historically famous precisely for its favourable effect on digestion and metabolism.

For international visitors, a little context helps: a Czech spa "cure" (*lázeňská kúra*) is a doctor-led programme, not a hotel package. It combines prescribed treatments, a dietary regime and medical supervision — closer to a residential health programme than to a wellness weekend.

## Springs that help digestion

At the heart of the metabolic cure are two springs: the **Cross Spring (Křížový pramen)** and the **Ferdinand Spring (Ferdinandův pramen)**. Both belong to the sulphur — more precisely, the sulphate — springs; they were once called "Glauber" or "saline" springs, because they contain Glauber's salt (sodium sulphate), which gives them a slightly bitter taste. And it is that salt that holds the key to their effect.

The Cross Spring is recognised for disorders of the digestive tract, metabolic disorders, obesity, gout and diabetes. Its sulphate component acts as a mild laxative, stimulates the intestine, gallbladder and pancreas, and helps the body to "retune" its digestion. The Ferdinand Spring works in much the same way and is used for the same indications.

One curiosity few people know is the influence of temperature: warm water from the Cross Spring is traditionally recommended for diarrhoea, cold water for constipation. It illustrates neatly why a drinking cure cannot be done by guesswork.

### How to take the drinking cure correctly

Here precision matters, because the drinking cure attracts more myths than facts. **The drinking cure is always individual and prescribed by a spa physician.** "The more the better" does not apply — quite the opposite.

With sulphate springs such as the Cross Spring, the laxative effect already sets in at roughly **three-quarters of a litre a day**. That is why the usual recommendation is nearer **half to three-quarters of a litre daily**, divided into small portions, generally sipped before meals. The idea that one should drink "litres of healing water" every day is not only mistaken but, with sulphur springs, medically risky. The water is drunk lukewarm, slowly, from the traditional spouted cup — and the exact volume and temperature are set by the physician according to your diagnosis.

The drinking cure, then, is not a weight-loss tool in the literal sense. It burns no fat. It helps bring digestion and metabolism into balance, which matters more for lasting weight regulation than any quick effect. We covered the drinking cure in more detail in our [drinking-cure guide](/en/magazine/pitna-kura-pruvodce).

## Movement as the second pillar

The metabolic cure stands on two legs — and the second is movement. Mariánské Lázně is made for it: the town sits at around 630 metres above sea level, ringed by forest, and has held the status of a climatic spa since 2023. The mild sub-montane climate makes walking easy without straining the body.

The colonnade, the promenades and the forest paths of the Slavkov Forest form a network of routes you walk naturally and gladly. A realistic daily target need not be a marathon — around **eight thousand steps a day** is widely regarded as a sensible wellness goal (not a medical prescription, but a reasonable benchmark). Combined with a regular eating rhythm and the calm the spa town offers, walking becomes the most effective and gentlest "medicine" for weight.

